\section{Introduction}

Danish has an everyday word for learning something so thoroughly that it
becomes part of oneself: \emph{at tilegne sig}, with the noun
\emph{Tilegnelse}. English has no counterpart. The usual rendering,
``appropriate,'' is assembled from the right parts---Latin \emph{ad} and
\emph{proprius}, ``toward'' and ``one's own''---but no English speaker hears
them, and no teacher tells a student that the material now needs to be
appropriated. The Danish word wears its meaning on its sleeve: \emph{til},
toward, and \emph{egen}, own. The word is old Danish---\emph{Ordbog over
det danske Sprog} (s.v.\ \emph{tilegne}) traces it to the Old Danish participle
\emph{til eynath} and refers the reader to \emph{gøre sig (noget) egent}, to
make (something) one's own. To \emph{tilegne} a book to someone is to make it theirs; to \emph{tilegne
sig} a truth is to make it one's own. Kierkegaard uses the word for ordinary
learning as readily as for his theory of truth:
``væsentligen lærer den Ældre kun ved at tilegne sig, og væsentligen tilegner
han sig kun det Væsentlige ved at gjøre det'' (\emph{Tre Taler ved tænkte
Leiligheder}, SKS 5, 417). A Danish reader of the \emph{Postscript} meets a
word from ordinary talk about learning, pressed into service as a theory of
truth; an English reader meets a technical term, and a translation that
varies with context---``appropriate'' here, ``make one's own'' or
``assimilate'' or ``internalize'' there---hides the single word that runs from
Sibbern through Kierkegaard to Høffding. The passages collected here trace
that word through four stages.

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Sibbern made \emph{Tilegnelse} an epistemic term.} In
  \emph{Om Erkjendelse og Granskning} (1822), conviction turns the mere
  reception of truth into ``en inderlig Tilegnelse,'' by which the truth
  ``kommer til kraftigen at boe i denne Individualitet'' (pp.~9--10), and free
  thinking makes the knower at home in what is known, ``som i sin egen Ejendom,
  hvori intet mere er den fremmed'' (p.~71). In \emph{Bemærkninger og
  Undersøgelser} (1838) he pairs the word with assimilation---``en dybere
  Tilegnelse og, saa at sige, Assimilation af det Sande'' (p.~102)---and warns
  that knowledge without the pathos of appropriation stands in us ``som et
  Skyggebillede \ldots\ en Reminiscents'' (pp.~109--111). For Sibbern,
  appropriation completes cognition: the more deeply one knows, the more fully
  the truth becomes one's own.

\item \textbf{Kierkegaard took over the term and separated it from knowing.}
  His dissertation already names the task of the age as carrying the results
  of science into personal life, ``personligt at tilegne sig disse'' (SKS 1,
  356). In the \emph{Postscript} the continuity Sibbern took for granted
  becomes the target. Objective thought assumes that once the truth is
  established, ``saa er det en smal Sag med Tilegnelsen, den følger af sig
  selv i Kjøbet'' (SKS 7, 30). Against this, ``det Væsentlige ved Erkjendelsen
  netop er Tilegnelsen'' (SKS 7, 79), and the definition of truth for an
  existing person puts appropriation exactly where objective knowledge gives
  out: ``den objektive Uvished, fastholdt i den meest lidenskabelige
  Inderligheds Tilegnelse, er Sandheden'' (SKS 7, 187). Appropriation is no
  longer the completion of knowledge but what the truth consists in.

\item \textbf{Kierkegaard also gave the term a species.} The \emph{Postscript}
  rejects the view that faith is appropriation ``saaledes, at den specifikt
  ikke er forskjellig fra anden intellectuel Tilegnelse'' (SKS 7, 553), and
  insists instead that ``det at troe er specifik bestemmet forskjelligt fra al
  anden Tilegnelse og Inderlighed'' (SKS 7, 555). Sibbern's appropriation is
  a genus with one direction of growth; Kierkegaard marks off a kind of
  appropriation that cannot be reached by knowing more. The same concept
  governs his account of communication: what the Sophists lacked was
  ``Tilegnelsens Moment,'' and ``Tilegnelsen er netop Samtalens Hemmelighed''
  (SKS 4, 323).

\item \textbf{Brøchner and Høffding read him this way.} Brøchner's summary in
  \emph{Problemet om Tro og Viden} (1868) is exact: ``Tilegnelsen er Opgaven,
  og det Væsentlige ikke et Hvad, men et Hvorledes \ldots\ Sandheden er
  Tilegnelsens Inderlighed, Sandheden er Subjectiviteten'' (p.~124). The
  young Høffding, breaking with Rasmus Nielsen in 1867, puts the lesson in the
  first person: ``kun hvad jeg personligt kan erfare og tilegne mig, kan være
  Sandhed for mig'' (\emph{Erindringer}, pp.~67--69).
\end{enumerate}

